# agents

The agent loop and prompt surface. Single source of truth for prompt
strings; the registry pattern keeps variant cells coupled to runtime.

## Must
- All mono prompt strings live in `src/luxe/agents/prompts.py` `PROMPT_REGISTRY`
- `TaskOverlay` maps `task_type` to `PromptVariant` id; routing goes through `resolve_prompt_ids`
- `run_agent` (loop.py) owns the inner tool-call loop; `single.py` is the entry
- `guardrails.py` is the SOLE home of intervention thresholds + nudge bodies
  (`_WRITE_PRESSURE_*`, `_PROSE_BURST_*`, `_ACTION_DENSITY_GATE_*`,
  `_EARLY_BAIL_*`, `_HABITUATION_EXIT_*`, `_POST_WRITE_IDLE_MAX`,
  `_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_REPEAT_STEPS`, `_BREADTH_PROBE_ESCALATION_COUNT`,
  `_CONVERGENCE_{LOW,HIGH}_THRESHOLD`, `_v1105_synthesis_looping_signature`)
  together with their calibration rationale. `loop.py` RE-EXPORTS them so the
  loop test modules' `from luxe.agents.loop import _X` keeps resolving;
  `tests/test_guardrails_identity.py` pins `loop.X is guardrails.X`. Never
  reintroduce a local definition — the 2026-05-26 extraction copied instead of
  moving and the two sets silently coexisted until 2026-08-04
- Mode B write-pressure injection (v1.4.1) is opt-in via `LUXE_WRITE_PRESSURE=1`
- Every `LUXE_*` switch `run_agent` obeys is read in `agents/flags.py`
  (`RunFlags.from_env`), once, at the top of the run — NOT with an
  `os.environ.get` in the body. `run_agent` assigns each field to the local
  name it always used, so the extraction changed no behaviour: same defaults,
  same "only the exact string 1" / "only the exact string 0" asymmetries, same
  silent fallback for every malformed value. A new switch adds a field there
  plus a row in `tests/test_run_flags.py`, and a test asserts the loop's body
  contains no `os.environ` access. Three reads deliberately stay outside:
  `LUXE_TOKEN_LOG_INTERVAL` (loop.py module constant, read at import),
  `LUXE_LOAD_PRIORS` (cohort_priors) and `LUXE_EARLY_BAIL_TRAJECTORY_SHAPE`
  (guardrails) — those belong to their own modules' contracts
- SpecDD Lever 2 chain block (`_build_sdd_block`) is appended after the variant's `task_prefix`

### Reflection / verify stage invariants (Track 1, pinned 2026-05-24)

- **reflect surface ownership**: `reflect.py` is the single source of truth for the
  verifier/critique prompt surface (the `_VERIFIER_SYSTEM` + per-driver templates). This
  is DISTINCT from `PROMPT_REGISTRY` (task-completion variants) by design — the verifier
  is a separate "second-look" critic, not a `run_agent` task prompt. New verifier prompt
  wording still requires a test in `tests/test_prompts.py` (the registry rule's intent).
- **opt-in + disable-equivalence**: the stage is gated by `LUXE_REFLECT` (default unset →
  off). With it off, generation is byte-identical (the primitive is pure; call sites gate).
- **verify-only is non-perturbing**: a verify call is a SEPARATE `backend.chat`; in
  verify-only mode it never mutates the work being judged. So Phase 1 can be measured
  offline by replaying it over stored transcripts without changing any benchmark verdict.
- **`gap` requires a substantiated FUNCTIONAL BLOCKER**: `gap=true` only when the model
  both asserts it AND supplies ≥1 deficiency; stylistic/suboptimal observations never flip
  it (defends the false-gap metric). Unparseable verdicts fail CLOSED (gap=false).
- **deterministic, no persistence**: temp=0; reflect must not read or write cross-run state.

### Phase 2 repair invariants (multi_turn; pinned 2026-05-24)

- **two-gate fire (give-up signature AND verify gap)**: the repair stage fires only when
  a turn produced ZERO tool calls (`adapter._is_giveup_turn` — the empty_turn give-up
  signature) AND a separate verify call returns `gap=true`. The structural gate goes
  FIRST — it gates the expensive verify call and, by construction, skips the verifier's
  reporting-gap false-gaps (which have non-empty action sets). A verify call is never
  made on a turn that already acted.
- **locked repair budget**: exactly ONE corrective re-prompt per flagged turn (a single
  `_luxe_repair`-marked user nudge + one bounded step pass over the SAME exposed tool
  surface). Hard stop: no re-verify, no second repair, no loop. The re-prompt's step loop is
  capped at `_REPAIR_MAX_STEPS` (=4) — TIGHTER than the initial turn's `_MAX_STEPS_PER_TURN`
  (=15) — to bound the over-action/runaway mode (the Phase-2 `miss_func_112` 6-step/51-call
  spiral); `_MAX_CALLS_PER_PROBLEM` still backstops the whole problem. The cap is
  artifact-scoped (covers every observed Phase-2 repair in the A/B archive), not re-benched.
- **same tool surface**: the repair re-prompt is driven with the turn's already-computed
  `openai_tools` (the per-turn exposed surface) — repair may NOT widen the surface or
  invoke tools outside the original trajectory's surface (pre-registered budget).
- **append-to-same-turn**: repair output extends `decoded_turns[-1]` (the give-up turn),
  never a new turn entry — turn count must stay aligned with the GT turn count.
- **generic nudge**: `reflect.repair_nudge` carries no benchmark semantics; it re-states
  the request as unfinished and lists the verifier's deficiencies (consuming the verdict,
  not re-solving). The `_luxe_repair` marker keeps the nudge out of later verify contexts.

### forge-hybrid Phase 2 (A) compaction invariants (pinned 2026-05-28, DEFAULT-ON)

- **TieredCompact ships default-ON** (`LUXE_TIERED_COMPACT` defaults to enabled; set to "0"
  to disable for ablation). The 4-arm n=75 sweep (OFF / 0.75 / 0.50 / 0.40) + 4-arm
  n=14 stress + 2-rep noise validation at phase_thresholds=(0.50, 0.85, 0.95) jointly
  established: resolve rate is equivalent to no-compaction baseline within substrate
  noise band (±2.8 at n=75); wall improvement is 42-56% (well outside substrate wall
  noise ~20%); 2 protected wrong_target instances (matplotlib-25775, pylint-6528) heal
  via phase-1 fires; zero new wrong_target damages. This is the cycle's only Pareto-
  positive lever.
- **Default phase_thresholds = (0.50, 0.85, 0.95)** owned by
  `luxe.context.TieredCompact._DEFAULT_PHASE_THRESHOLDS`. Aggressive phase 1 (50% pressure)
  captures protective phase-1 wins (drop _luxe_nudge + truncate tool_results); conservative
  phase 3 (95% pressure) avoids the destructive reasoning-drop mode (observed at 1/75
  firing rate at this threshold). The phase-1-helps / phase-3-hurts split is the cycle's
  load-bearing design finding.
- **Strict counter discipline**: `writes_seen`, `reads`, `tool_calls_total`, and all
  `*_fired` flags are explicit integer/boolean state owned by `run_agent`. They are
  NEVER recomputed by scanning `messages`. Compaction can drop arbitrary message-level
  content from the eligible region; loop state must stay consistent under compaction.
  This is a regression guard against any future "lazy fix" that derives counters from
  message inspection.
- **Marker-based nudge identification**: TieredCompact identifies droppable nudges
  via the `_luxe_nudge` (and `_luxe_repair`) keys on message dicts, NOT by body-text
  matching. Body-text matching is FORBIDDEN — it's fragile under message-text drift.
  Every guardrail that injects a nudge MUST tag the message with `_luxe_nudge=True` and
  `_luxe_nudge_type="<guard_name>"`. See `docs/luxe-markers-audit.md`.
- **Protected messages**: `messages[0]` (system prompt) and `messages[1]` (original
  task) are NEVER eligible for compaction. The last `keep_recent` assistant-message
  iterations are also protected. Compaction operates only on messages indexed
  `[2, eligible_end)`.

### Server-truth context calibration (pinned 2026-08-11, DEFAULT-ON, UNBENCHED)

- `LUXE_CTX_SERVER_TRUTH` (`RunFlags.ctx_server_truth`, default ON; only the
  exact string "0" disables, the tiered_compact spelling). After each response
  the loop computes `calibration_ratio(resp.timing.prompt_tokens, est_sent)`
  and folds it into the DENOMINATOR via `calibrated_ctx_limit` — every
  consumer (`context_pressure`, `TieredCompact.compact`,
  `elide_old_tool_results`) divides by `effective_ctx` and inherits the
  correction without learning about it. **The phase thresholds keep their
  pinned (0.50, 0.85, 0.95) values** — this changes what the fraction MEANS,
  never the fraction.
- Why: `estimate_tokens` is `len(text) // 4`, which runs far low on the code
  and JSON tool payloads an agent loop actually carries. Measured 2026-08-11:
  1.84x / 1.92x / 2.24x across three turns of session 0e524f033300, and
  **2.38x-3.69x** through a live 13-step log-analysis run (a 576 KB file read
  in windows), where `est=19.8%` against a true `47.3%`. So the phases were
  firing somewhere near 1.0-1.9x of the real window — phases 2 and 3 could not
  fire at all before the server rejected the prompt outright. The status bar
  was corrected to server truth in June (`last_prompt_tokens / num_ctx`); the
  thresholds were not, and the two have disagreed by 2x+ ever since.
- The ratio is NOT a constant to hardcode: it drifts within a run (3.69x at
  step 1 down to 2.38x by step 13 as prose displaces the tool schemas the
  estimate misses hardest) and varies by workload. That is why it is
  re-measured every step rather than latched or tabulated.
- Step 1 is always uncalibrated (nothing to calibrate against) and matches the
  historical reading exactly. Recalibrated EVERY step rather than latched: the
  prose/code/JSON mix shifts as tool payloads accumulate.
- `calibration_ratio` degrades to 1.0 on a missing or zero `prompt_tokens` —
  a backend reporting no usage falls back to the estimate, never to a wrong
  number — and is clamped to [0.5, 8.0] so one malformed usage block cannot
  swing compaction to an extreme.
- **UNBENCHED, and the specific risk is phase 3.** Measured 2026-08-11 on a
  synthetic 20-step trajectory at num_ctx=32768: the OLD reading gave 37.7%
  pressure and fired **phase 0** (nothing compacted); the same messages at the
  real-world cal=2.4x give 90.6% and fire **phase 2** (tool results dropped,
  all 20 assistant texts preserved); at the 8.0x clamp ceiling they give 302%
  and fire **phase 3**, which drops 17 of 20 assistant reasoning messages.
  Phase 3 is the mode `agents.sdd` already records as destructive (it "DESTROY[s]
  existing patches"; the phase-1-helps/phase-3-hurts split is the compaction
  cycle's load-bearing finding), and until now it was effectively DEAD CODE —
  reaching 0.95 on the estimate required ~228% real context, which the server
  rejects first. Calibration makes it reachable for the first time.
- That reads as a hazard and is probably the opposite. A high calibration means
  the prompt genuinely does not fit: at cal=8 an est of 12,368 is ~98,944 real
  tokens against a 32,768 window, so the OLD behaviour was to compact nothing
  and send an overflowing prompt into `validate_context_window`'s HTTP 400 —
  i.e. a **context_overflow bailout**. Phase 3 dropping reasoning is the
  correct response to a context that has actually overflowed; failing the
  request is not. **Testable prediction for the bench run: context_overflow
  bailouts should DECREASE.** (Suggestive prior: C10's own writeup noted "one
  recovered context_overflow bailout" as the only delta between two arms that
  were byte-identical requests — i.e. that recovery was noise, and the class
  exists in this suite.) Do NOT lower `CALIBRATION_MAX` to suppress phase 3
  without first checking that number; the clamp exists to bound a malformed
  usage report, not to keep a legitimate overflow response from firing.
- `LUXE_CTX_SERVER_TRUTH=0` restores the pre-2026-08-11 behaviour exactly; run
  maintain_suite before treating the new default as settled.

### forge-hybrid Phase 3 (B) respond-terminal invariants (pinned 2026-05-28, opt-in)

- **`LUXE_RESPOND_TERMINAL` defaults OFF.** The B1+B2 smoke at n=14 confirmed the
  champion does NOT call the respond tool organically (0/14 adoption) and does NOT
  call it under explicit prompt guidance (0/14 adoption with `swebench_bugfix_respond`).
  The lever is in-tree as infrastructure but does not engage on this champion; future
  cycles may re-attempt if a different champion is fielded.
- **4 watchdog gates** when the flag IS set: (1) compaction-phantom (Phase ≥2 + writes==0),
  (2) early-respond (writes==0 + step<4), (3) no-writes-late (writes==0 + step≥4),
  (4) passive-surrender (writes≥1 + step≤last_write_step). First match wins; non-terminal
  gates inject typed reprompt + continue.

### forge-hybrid Phase 4 (D) trajectory-shape invariants (pinned 2026-05-28, opt-in)

- **`LUXE_EARLY_BAIL_TRAJECTORY_SHAPE` defaults OFF.** The D smoke at n=14 confirmed
  the locked predicate (`sustained_low_trend ≥ 3 AND grep_vs_read_ratio < 0.5 AND
  breadth_saturation < 0.6`) is too narrow for this champion at num_ctx=32768: 0/14
  suppression fires across the smoke set. The lever is in-tree as infrastructure but
  the predicate does not engage; future cycles may trace-mine to find empirically-grounded
  thresholds.
- **Implicit dependency on `LUXE_ADAPTIVE_POLICY=1`** for `score_log` population. With
  adaptive policy off, the predicate is a no-op (sustained_low_trend = 0). This is a
  documented coupling, not a bug.

### Tool-call telemetry events (pinned 2026-08-04, additive-only)

- `tool_reject` (reason=schema|unknown_tool, name + message) and
  `textfallback_drop` (names + recovered) land in events.jsonl, gated by
  `log_calls` like every other event. They are TELEMETRY: they must never
  add/remove/alter a `messages` entry, change dispatch, or gate control flow.
  The 2026-08 taxonomy refuted every hardening candidate, so observation
  shipped without behavior change — keep it that way unless a future
  evidence-cleared cycle says otherwise.
- `_parse_text_tool_calls(drops=)` collects dropped candidate names
  (deduplicated — the `<tool_call>` block and the bare-JSON scanner see the
  same candidate); the default `drops=None` call is byte-identical to the
  pre-2026-08-04 signature.
- `scripts/toolcall_taxonomy.py` prefers these direct events and suppresses
  its legacy proxies per-call/per-run; if either event's fields change,
  update the script's suppression paths in the same commit.

### Post-write idle repeat counting (pinned 2026-08-10, opt-in, UNBENCHED)

- `LUXE_POST_WRITE_IDLE_REPEATS=1` (`RunFlags.post_write_idle_repeats`) makes a
  post-write REPEAT call — same tool, same arguments as an earlier call this
  run — count toward the `post_write_idle_tools` streak instead of resetting
  it. **Default OFF; with it unset the loop is byte-identical.**
- Why it exists: `PostWriteIdleExitGuard`'s docstring always claimed it caught
  non-write calls that "hit the dedup short-circuit", but `read_file` is in
  `_DEDUP_EXEMPT_TOOLS`, so a repeated read returns full content, resets the
  streak, and the guard never arms. `consecutive_repeat` is blind for the same
  reason. The gap is between the guard's stated contract and what it can see.
- Demonstrated on m1, 2026-08-10, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-4bit, from
  `~/.luxe/runs/smoke-code/events.jsonl`: step 1 reads key `f0ee19e9`; after
  the edit at step 9, step 10 reads the SAME key again, recorded
  `dup=False` (the exemption) with `bytes=78`. Under the old logic that call
  RESET the streak; with the switch on it counts, and `post_write_idle_exit`
  fires on that run where it does not on the identical runs beside it.
- **This is a latent gap, not the cause of any known abort.** It was found
  while investigating m1's code-drill failure, but that failure was a step
  BUDGET problem — seven bash probes before the edit at step 9, 14 calls over
  13 steps against a 12-step cap — fixed by `_CODE_DRILL_STEPS_LOW_BIT`, not
  by this switch. An earlier draft of this section attributed a
  seven-repeated-reads trajectory to m1; that came from misreading a stale
  run directory and is withdrawn.
- `call_is_repeat` is captured BEFORE the dedup branch and before
  `seen_calls.add(key)`, so it means "this exact call already ran". It is
  deliberately distinct from `step_had_repeat`, which the dedup exemption
  keeps False for `read_file` — the very tool that created the blind spot.
- A NOVEL post-write call still resets the streak. Exploration after a write
  must not be cut short; only redundant work counts.
- **BENCHED 2026-08-10 — default STAYS OFF.** 3 reps x 10 fixtures x 2 arms
  on the champion: 27/30 and 111/150 in BOTH arms, per-fixture scores
  identical across all six passes, `post_write_idle_exit` 9 in both. The
  switch is **inert on this suite**, not merely neutral: across 60 runs the
  max post-write repeats in any single run is 1, against a threshold of 3, so
  a lone repeat can never arm the guard. The wall/token deltas are substrate
  noise — no firing decision changed. Report:
  `acceptance/pwir_ab_2026_08_10/REPORT.md`.
- Venue check done offline against 1,369 archived trajectories, 2026-08-10
  (`acceptance/pwir_ab_2026_08_10/SWEBENCH-VENUE-CHECK.md`). **SWE-bench is the
  WRONG venue**: the switch would change 7 of 896 runs (0.8%), and 0 of the 136
  most recent. An n=75 A/B there would expect under one affected instance.
- **maintain_suite is now the right venue, and the earlier "do not re-run this
  suite" note is withdrawn.** 9 of 154 August runs (5.8%) would change, all on
  `nothing-ever-happens-document-config` — the fixture with documented temp=0
  variance. The A/B's own 60 runs really did have zero opportunity (re-checked,
  0/60), so its verdict stands; the pattern shows up in later runs.
- Careful with the reasoning, not just the verdict: a lone repeat CAN arm the
  guard by topping up an existing 2-streak (`baseline_streak=1 →
  treatment_streak=3` in all 9 cases). REPORT.md's claim that it "can never arm
  the guard by itself" was an overstatement of a correct empirical result.
- **RE-BENCHED 2026-08-10 and REFUTED.** With opportunity present (3 runs per
  arm had a streak reaching 3 only when repeats count) the switch changed
  nothing: 30/30 and 120/150 in BOTH arms, `post_write_idle_exit` 12 in both
  with an identical per-fixture distribution, wall identical to 0.1s, tokens
  +0.3%. The predicted wall/token saving did not materialise because in those
  runs the guard already armed via the zero-byte path. Report:
  `acceptance/pwir_rerun_2026_08_10/REPORT.md`.
- Disposition: keep in-tree, default OFF, documented as refuted — same as
  `LUXE_RESPOND_TERMINAL` and the trajectory-shape suppressor. **Do not
  re-bench on maintain_suite**; two 3-rep A/Bs from opposite starting
  conditions (no opportunity, then opportunity) agree. Reviving it needs a
  workload where post-write repeat streaks reach 3 WITHOUT zero-byte calls
  already getting there.

### Truncated-turn retry (pinned 2026-08-10, DEFAULT-ON, BENCHED)

- `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=1` (`RunFlags.truncated_turn_retry`): when a turn
  returns `finish_reason == "length"` AND carries no tool call, replay the
  cut-off text into history, append `_TRUNCATED_TURN_MESSAGE`, and CONTINUE
  instead of taking the terminal path. Bounded by
  `_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` (2). **DEFAULT-ON since 2026-08-10** — only
  the exact string "0" disables it, the tiered_compact spelling. Set
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0` for ablation.
- Why: the loop's terminal test is `if not tool_calls:` and it never consulted
  `finish_reason`, so a turn CUT OFF mid-sentence was indistinguishable from a
  model that finished and chose to answer. The run ended `aborted=False` with
  no diff and no gate fired.
- Founding instance: lpe-rope-calc-implement-strict-flag, 6/6 identical runs,
  exactly 8,192 completion tokens (the cap), cut off mid-word, scored 1/5 for
  "produced no diff" while reporting a clean completion. Neither
  write_pressure (needs >=10 tool calls; had 3) nor prose_burst (needs 0; had
  3) can see that shape.
- ONLY `"length"` triggers it. `"stop"` is a model that chose to end, and
  retrying that would change behaviour on every ordinary completion in the
  suite; an unknown reason is not evidence of truncation. A truncated turn that
  DID make a tool call is left alone — it already acted.
- **A/B verdict (2026-08-10): all four pre-registered criteria met.** 3 reps x
  10 fixtures x 2 arms: 27/30 -> 30/30, score 111 -> 120, zero regressions,
  tokens +22.5% / wall +7.8% (ceiling was ~2x), and 3/3 firings landed on
  genuine cap hits with zero spurious fires. `terminal_turn_truncated` went
  3 -> 0 while `truncated_turn_retry` went 0 -> 3; all recovered on retry #1.
  Report: `acceptance/truncated_turn_ab_2026_08_10/REPORT.md`.
- Promoted to default-ON on that evidence (user decision, 2026-08-10). Caveat
  recorded because it governs any future re-tune: the mechanism fired 3 times,
  all on ONE fixture. The no-harm result is broad (27 untouched runs); the
  it-helps result is n=1 fixture, because the suite contains exactly one
  capped-turn `implement` trajectory. **If a workload behaves unexpectedly,
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0` is the first thing to try** — wider evidence
  than one fixture has never been gathered, and SWE-bench is where it would
  come from.
- `terminal_turn_truncated` is UNGATED telemetry (additive-only, per the
  telemetry section above): it fires in both arms and is the only record that
  distinguishes "finished" from "cut off". Keep it ungated whatever the
  default becomes.
- **The bound is configurable** (2026-08-11):
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` -> `RunFlags.truncated_turn_max_retries`,
  default `_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` (2, the benched value). Non-negative
  int; anything malformed degrades silently to the default, like every other
  knob in `flags.py`. Passed to `TruncatedTurnGuard.should_fire(max_retries=)`
  and into the `truncated_turn_retry` event, so the records say what bound was
  in force rather than assuming the constant.
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES=0` is NOT the same as
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0`: the mechanism stays enabled, so
  `terminal_turn_truncated` still reports `retry_enabled=True` and an ablation
  stays distinguishable from a tightened leash in the corpus.
  **Why it exists**: each retry is a full capped generation — ~2.5 min at
  8,192 tokens on the champion — and in chat the capped turn is frequently the
  model rambling rather than mid-edit, so the default spends ~8 min before the
  turn ends. Observed in session 0e524f033300 (2026-08-11), the first sighting
  of this mechanism outside the bench. Lowering the bound for chat is a
  judgement call, NOT a benched change: the 30/30 result was measured at 2 and
  the benchmark/maintain path must keep reading the unset default.
- `on_notice` (`run_agent`/`run_single`, default `None`) is the DISPLAY-ONLY
  seam that announces a retry to a front-end. It never influences control
  flow, is never consulted for a decision, and is wrapped so a raising
  callback cannot kill a run. Chat wires it; the benchmark/maintain path
  passes nothing and is unchanged. It exists because the retry was visible
  only in `events.jsonl` — a file nobody reads mid-turn — while it spent
  minutes behind a spinner. The terminal `finish_reason == "length"` case
  notifies too, whatever the switch: a turn reported as an answer when it was
  actually cut off is the exact confusion this whole mechanism was built for.

### Empty-completion retry (pinned 2026-08-17, DEFAULT-ON, UNBENCHED)

- `LUXE_EMPTY_TURN_RETRY` (`RunFlags.empty_turn_retry`, DEFAULT-ON; only the
  exact string "0" disables it, the tiered_compact spelling): when a terminal
  turn carries NO tool call and NO text, append `_EMPTY_TURN_MESSAGE` and
  CONTINUE instead of taking the terminal path. Bounded by
  `_EMPTY_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` (1). Same mechanism, same shape and the same
  reasoning as truncated-turn retry above — it is deliberately modelled on it.
- Why: the terminal test is `if not tool_calls:`, and an empty `resp.text`
  satisfies it exactly as a real answer does. A blank reply was therefore
  recorded as a finished turn — `aborted=False`, steps=1, no gate, no warning,
  and nothing in the record separating "answered" from "said nothing".
- Founding instance: session 0eb5998d8825 turn -5 (2026-08-17), a hosted
  REASONING model (`moonshotai/kimi-k3`): steps=1, tool_calls=0, normal finish
  reason, `content` empty — the model spent its budget in the reasoning
  channel and emitted no content at all. The shape is NOT reasoning-specific
  (any provider hiccup produces it), so the guard keys on the observable
  emptiness, never on the model.
- Whitespace-only counts as empty: a lone newline is not an answer.
  `finish_reason == "length"` is EXCLUDED — that is truncation, which
  `TruncatedTurnGuard` owns with its own message and its own larger budget,
  and it is ordered first so the two never spend both budgets on one response.
- ONE retry. A model that returns nothing twice will not answer on a third,
  and each attempt is a full generation.
- **This reaches the BENCHMARK path, intentionally**, exactly as truncated-turn
  retry did: a bench run that records an empty answer as a completion is
  measuring the wrong thing. **UNBENCHED** — no maintain_suite A/B has been
  run. If a workload behaves unexpectedly, `LUXE_EMPTY_TURN_RETRY=0` joins
  `LUXE_TIERED_COMPACT=0` and `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0` as a first thing
  to try.
- `terminal_turn_empty` is UNGATED telemetry (additive-only): it fires in both
  arms and is the only record distinguishing an answer from silence. It
  carries `reasoning_chars`, because "thought for 3,568 characters and said
  nothing" and "returned nothing" are different failures with different fixes.
  Keep it ungated whatever the default becomes.
- `on_notice` announces it live, same seam and same rules as above.

### Intermediate-step prose (`AgentResult.step_texts`, 2026-08-17)

- A step that emits text ALONGSIDE tool calls appends that text to
  `result.step_texts`. Purely additive reporting, like `last_prompt_tokens`:
  `final_text` keeps its exact meaning (the LAST step's text), no loop logic
  reads the new field, and no benchmark grader sees anything new.
- It exists because models routinely put the opening of their answer on an
  acting step ("Let me check the config…", or a whole lead-in paragraph) and
  chat rendered `final_text` alone, so replies began mid-thought (session
  0eb5998d8825 turn -7: 3 steps, 2 tool calls, the lead-in stranded in step 1).
  The JOIN is done in the chat layer only (`render.compose_answer`) — do not
  fold it into `final_text` here, or every grader's input changes.

## Must not (reflect)

- Encode benchmark semantics in verifier prompts (e.g. "a tool call was warranted") —
  evaluator-shaped phrasing is forbidden (anti-overfitting). The multi_turn verifier asks
  about the *request being carried out*, never about tool calls.
- Let verify-only mutate the generation transcript, or let Phase 2 repair invoke tools
  outside the original trajectory's tool surface (pre-registered repair budget).

### Stage 3 / v1.11 adaptive-policy invariants (pinned 2026-05-20)

- **score_log ownership**: `loop.py` owns the per-step convergence-score deque; `convergence.py` (`compute_convergence_score`) stays stateless. Composition boundary is the `_compute_within_run_state(score_log, tool_history, step)` signature — loop.py is the sole writer, convergence.py is the sole consumer
- **Bias-not-lock**: no signal combination may reduce intervention probability to zero or increase it above the configured ceiling. Adaptive policy modulates intervention timing/intensity, never enables/disables. Property-tested in Phase 1
- **Slew-rate limit**: per-step intensity-modifier delta bounded by `LUXE_ADAPTIVE_MAX_INTENSITY_DELTA_PER_STEP` (default 0.3). Prevents oscillation; makes regressions smoother instead of catastrophic
- **Disable-equivalence**: `LUXE_ADAPTIVE_POLICY=0` (or unset) produces byte-identical behavior to v1.10.5
- **Priors are log-only in v1.11**: `LUXE_LOAD_PRIORS=1` reads `~/.luxe/cohort-history/<instance>.json` and logs to events.jsonl, but does **not** influence intervention intensity (deferred to v1.11.1+)

### Stage 3 / v1.11 Phase B activation — TRIED + REVERTED (2026-05-21)

- **no_write bias RETIRED (KEPT)**: the no_write → write_pressure/early_bail bias is pinned at 0.0. Phase A (`project_v111_phaseA_calibration`) proved no_write non-selective (precision ≤31% — read-heavy successes hit the same depths as stalls). This is behaviorally v1.10.5-neutral (write_pressure reverts to its static schedule) and stays.
- **score_trend → soft_anchor collapse promotion: REVERTED**. The Phase B lever raised the score<LOW band response one rung (breadth_probe → soft_anchor commitment nudge) on the confirmed-collapse signal `conv<LOW AND step>=_COLLAPSE_MIN_STEP AND trend<=0`. Phase D (n=75 ×3) showed it **net-negative**: it fired 3/3 on xarray-3305 (strong→plausible) and pylint-4661 (plausible→wrong_target), demoting tier via PREMATURE COMMITMENT, with 0 conversions and 0 Docker gain. cohort_shift_3x3 = 3 deterministic losses → HOLD. The loop.py consumer was reverted; the bias is retained as observability only.
- **Root-cause lesson for v1.11.1**: the step-`_COLLAPSE_MIN_STEP` collapse signature cannot distinguish "truly stalled" from "mid-deep-dive with a transient low-trend dip", so the commitment nudge derails recovering trajectories. The `trend<=0` Pareto guard was necessary but NOT sufficient. A usable stall signal needs non-recovery specificity (sustained trend<=0 over K steps, or a semantic-breadth-saturation signal — the "breadth not temporal counters" direction) — NOT a single-step snapshot.
- The "intensity-not-enable" framing (selecting a band-response message variant for an already-triggered early_bail predicate) remains a valid reading of the bias-only Must-not, should a future lever reuse the promotion mechanism with a better gate.

## Must not
- Inline prompt strings in `single.py` or `cli.py` (un-couples variants from runtime)
- Add feature flags or backwards-compatibility shims for retired modes (swarm / micro / phased)
- Add a new prompt variant without a corresponding test in `tests/test_prompts.py`
- (v1.11) Mutate the score_log from `convergence.py` — ownership lives in `loop.py`
- (v1.11) Let a Stage 3 signal hard-gate (enable/disable) an intervention dispatch — bias only
- (v1.11) Wire cross-cycle priors into intervention intensity computation — log-only this cycle
- (v1.11.1+) Ship `intervention_history_within_run` without capping its weight below all other Stage 3 signals AND restricting it to suppress-only (never amplify) — prevents intervention-aversion attractor

## Owns
- src/luxe/agents/**
